\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Values and sizes determine the one-edge polynomial}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{Let #{B} be a \vocabk{double-coset kernel}{fdip-012C}, with
value #{\beta_j} on the #{j}-th double coset, and let that
coset have size #{s_j}. Let #{\mathcal K} be the
\vocabk{class-function kernel}{fdip-012C} with value #{2} at
the identity and value #{1} at every other element. On
functions constant on the double cosets, the
\vocabk{one-edge operator}{fdip-012C} has matrix
#{D+pc^{\top}} in the indicator basis. Here #{D} is the
diagonal matrix of the squares #{\beta_j^2}, the column #{p}
has entries #{\beta_j}, and the column #{c} has entries
#{s_j\beta_j}. The diagonal entry is #{\beta_j^2(1+s_j)}, and
the off-diagonal entry from #{j} to #{m} is
#{\beta_m s_j\beta_j}. The characteristic polynomial is
therefore determined by the values #{\beta_j} and the sizes
#{s_j}. The rank equals the number of double cosets, and
\ref{fdip-013G} equates that number with the sum of the
squares of the invariant dimensions.}

\proof{
\p{For this kernel,
##{(C_{\mathcal K}f)(g)=f(g)+\sum_{x\in G}f(x),}
because the identity term is counted once in the sum and the
value #{2} adds it once more. Therefore
##{(\transferop f)(g)
 =B(g)^2 f(g)+B(g)\sum_{x\in G}B(x)f(x).}
On the indicator of the #{j}-th coset the inner sum equals
#{s_j\beta_j}. On the #{m}-th coset the value is
#{\beta_m^2} when #{m=j}, together with
#{\beta_m s_j\beta_j} for every pair. Those are the entries
of #{D+pc^{\top}}. Each entry is a polynomial in the values
and the sizes, so the characteristic polynomial, the
determinant of #{t{\bbI}} minus that matrix, is determined by
the same data. \ref{fdip-012D} shows that the operator
preserves this space and that the indicators form a basis, so
the rank is the number of double cosets. \ref{fdip-013G}
identifies that number with the sum of the squares of the
invariant dimensions.}
}
